for decades the business known as claire's and there's numero numerous other businesses that also provide uh ear piercing services in cheyenne whether it be beauty shops places like adora day spa they've been providing a service to the citizens of cheyenne in a manner that has been decades long that has a decades-long track record of being safe under the proposed ordinance changes this service would no longer be available to most miners as it has been for as long as i can remember claire's being open under the current ordinance 568-010 the definition of body art excludes piercings of the outer perimeter of the lobe of the ear using a pre-sterilized single-use stud and clasp ear piercing system also known as a piercing gun in other words it is legal to use a piercing gun to pierce a child's ear lobes this exemption the earlobe piercings is also in the definition of body piercing and

it's also in the definitions uh in the state statutes as well removing this exception would now mean that a simple earlobe piercing using a piercing gun would now be considered body art to be clear tattoo shops do not perform body art on any person minor or otherwise who does not present proper id as outlined in the state statute miners must also have an accompanying parent and parent to sign a release on behalf of minors piercing earlobes with a piercing gun is the only exception to the id requirement because it's not defined in body art the state statute is uh 14-3-107 it says no person shall knowingly perform body art upon or under the skin of a person who has not reached the age of majority so

i want to make sure it's clear there is no set age limit doesn't say 16 or 14 or 12 says not of the age of majority except with the consent of the person's parent or legal guardian who is present at the time the body out procedure is performed upon the person the person performing body art procedure shall demand proof of age prior to administering the body art procedure upon the person a motor vehicle driver's license a registration card issued under the federal selective service an identity card identification card issued to a member of the armed forces a valid united states passport a tribal identification card issued by the governing body of the eastern shoshone wyoming tribe nowhere in the statute does it say including but not limited to uh

it doesn't say like or similar it's very specific which ids are allowed um for as long as i've been in business we've never accepted a student id as proof of id because it's not listed in the state statute so again to pierce a minor and accept a student id would not be only unlawful in my opinion it's unethical if someone came in and one of the peers saying a face piercing or a tattoo even on someone who was 12 years old if we were allowed to accept student ids i think certain shops would take advantage of that and do piercings and tattoos on people that have no business having that done and and sometimes uh

you'd be surprised what parents are willing to let their children do um if a simple earlobe piercing done with a piercing gun is to be considered body art under the city ordinances this would now be a violation of wyoming statutes wyoming statutes to find body art the same way that the city ordinance currently does so it would at least be conflicting with the city's uh the city's new ordinances piercing a minor child's earlobes would uh be legal in every part of wyoming except cheyenne if this ordinance does go through that begs the question why do we want to make a service that has been proven safe and i spoke to the health department asked if there was a state of infections or anything recently uh

